import{_ as e,o,c as a,ag as s}from"./chunks/framework.DyZQ0-wY.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"How the bot chooses your feet","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"footnotes/footing.md","filePath":"footnotes/footing.md"}'),r={name:"footnotes/footing.md"};function n(h,t,i,d,l,f){return o(),a("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[s('<h1 id="how-the-bot-chooses-your-feet" tabindex="-1">How the bot chooses your feet <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-the-bot-chooses-your-feet" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How the bot chooses your feet&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The dancing bot doesn&#39;t decide one step at a time. When a chart loads, Footnotes looks at the whole chart and chooses a foot for every arrow, picking the combination that&#39;s easiest to dance from start to finish. Rhythm games call this the chart&#39;s parity, and Footnotes works it out with SMEditor&#39;s <a href="./../parity.html">parity checker</a> and the rules below.</p><h2 id="the-rules" tabindex="-1">The rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The rules&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>One foot per arrow, two for a jump.</strong> Each arrow is stepped by one foot. When two arrows start together, the bot usually lands one foot on each.</li><li><strong>Two feet never share an arrow.</strong> If one foot is standing on an arrow, putting the other foot on it doesn&#39;t press it again. So when the same arrow comes twice in a row, the foot that&#39;s already on it steps it again. On the bot, that foot disappears for a moment and lands again on the beat.</li><li><strong>Move as little as you can.</strong> Every move takes effort, more the farther a foot travels and much more the faster it has to get there. The bot keeps a foot where it is when it can, and plans ahead: if an arrow is coming back soon, it leaves a foot near it. <a href="#how-effort-is-counted">How effort is counted</a> has the details.</li><li><strong>Free the foot on Up or Down.</strong> All footing styles plan ahead so one foot can leave Up or Down before the other has to swing from Left to Right or back. This also applies on doubles when the two side arrows lie on opposite sides of the planted foot. A sustained hold or a foot assignment in the chart editor can still require the swing.</li><li><strong>Alternate when it&#39;s comfortable.</strong> Left, right, left, right is the default, as long as each foot can stay on its own side of the pad.</li><li><strong>The same foot twice is fine when it&#39;s slow.</strong> When steps are 0.4 seconds or more apart, the bot would rather step twice with the same foot than cross your legs over.</li><li><strong>Favor alternating in fast passages.</strong> At intervals of 0.2 seconds or less, the bot prefers to alternate. It can still take two steps with one foot to free the other foot from Up or Down.</li><li><strong>Face the screen.</strong> Minimal effort, Natural and Alternating keep the dancer facing the screen. If the plan puts the left foot on Right and the right foot on Left, the bot looks for a route that adds no fast steps for either foot.</li><li><strong>Keep holds pressed.</strong> The shoe stays in contact with the panel through the end of the hold.</li><li><strong>Use the heel and toes together when needed.</strong> If three arrows fall together, or a jump comes during a hold, one foot can cover two neighboring panels. When one hold ends exactly as a neighboring hold begins, the foot can also pivot with the heel on one panel and the toes on the other.</li></ol><p>On four-panel singles, <strong>Free style</strong> can turn 90° or 180° during the intro, turn sideways during a break, or follow charted taps and jumps into a half-turn. It chooses at most one turn, and only when the shoes and body have time to rotate and the whole chart has fewer rows marked <strong>Fast steps</strong> at your playback rate. After a half-turn, the left foot can stand on Right and the right foot on Left with the legs side by side. The arrow labels still name the physical foot. The dancer faces the screen again after the song.</p><h2 id="how-fast-is-slow" tabindex="-1">How fast is slow? <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-fast-is-slow" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How fast is slow?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>What the bot does depends on the time between two steps:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Time between steps</th><th>What the bot does</th><th>For example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.4 s or more</td><td>Steps twice with the same foot rather than crossing over</td><td>Quarter notes up to 150 BPM, eighth notes up to 75 BPM</td></tr><tr><td>0.2 s to 0.4 s</td><td>Weighs both, leaning more toward alternating as it speeds up</td><td>Quarter notes from 150 to 300 BPM, eighth notes from 75 to 150 BPM</td></tr><tr><td>0.2 s or less</td><td>Alternates, and crosses over when it has to</td><td>Eighth notes from 150 BPM, sixteenth notes from 75 BPM</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="how-effort-is-counted" tabindex="-1">How effort is counted <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-effort-is-counted" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How effort is counted&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each time a foot moves to another arrow, the bot counts the effort of the move:</p><p><strong>effort = 50 × distance + 2 × speed²</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Distance</strong> is in panels: about 1.4 to an arrow next to the one the foot is on, and 2 to the opposite arrow.</li><li><strong>Speed</strong> is the distance divided by the time that foot has had since its own last step. When your feet take turns, each foot has twice the time between steps.</li></ul><p>The first part makes every move cost something, so feet stay put when they can. The second part grows with the square of the speed, like the energy of swinging your leg, so long, fast moves cost the most.</p><p>Minimal effort and Free style add 60 for any move straight across the pad. For example, a foot can still move from Down to Up while the other stays on Right. The separate rule about swinging around a foot planted on Up or Down applies at every tempo.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Move</th><th>Time the foot has</th><th>Effort</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stepping again where it is</td><td>any</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>To a next-door arrow</td><td>1 s</td><td>75</td></tr><tr><td>To a next-door arrow</td><td>0.5 s</td><td>87</td></tr><tr><td>To a next-door arrow</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>135</td></tr><tr><td>Across the pad (Left–Right)</td><td>0.5 s</td><td>192</td></tr><tr><td>Across the pad (Left–Right)</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>288</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The bot adds these up, together with the costs of crossing over, turning, and stepping twice with the same foot, and picks the footing with the smallest total for the whole chart.</p><h2 id="examples" tabindex="-1">Examples <a class="header-anchor" href="#examples" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Examples&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Take <strong>Left, Down, Right, Down</strong> as quarter notes at 120 BPM, half a second apart. Strictly alternating feet would send your left foot across your right leg to reach the Right arrow. The bot steps Left and Down with the left foot instead, and Right with the right foot, so your legs never cross.</p><p>Even as eighth notes at 150 BPM, the bot can use the left foot twice to free the right foot for Right. If Down is a hold and both side arrows must be hit during that hold, the free foot takes both side steps.</p><p>Say your left foot is on Down and your right foot on Right, and then Up and Right come together. The bot moves only your left foot, from Down across the pad to Up, and your right foot stays on Right. Moving both feet instead, with the right foot to Up and the left foot to Right, would avoid crossing the pad but cross your legs over.</p><p>Take <strong>Left, Right, Down, Up, Down, Up, Left, Down</strong>, half a second apart. Your right foot could take Up and your left foot Down, but then the last Down would mean swinging the right foot across the pad from Up to Down. The bot plans ahead: your right foot takes Down and your left foot takes Up, so the last Down is already under your right foot. That&#39;s 4.2 panels of travel instead of 6.2, for one extra step with the same foot: the left foot steps Up and then Left.</p><h2 id="the-footing-setting" tabindex="-1">The Footing setting <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-footing-setting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Footing setting&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <strong>Footing</strong> menu in the bottom bar lets you choose a footing style while you practice. The chart editor has the same setting in <strong>Preferences &gt; Parity &gt; Footing</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>Minimal effort</strong> (the default) favors short, low-speed steps while facing the screen.</li><li><strong>Free style</strong> uses Minimal effort scoring and can turn on four-panel singles to reduce fast steps.</li><li><strong>Natural</strong> faces the screen and gives less weight to foot travel than Minimal effort does, including moves across the pad. It alternates feet more and plans ahead less. It was the default before Minimal effort.</li><li><strong>Alternating</strong> prefers to alternate feet, even in slow passages. It can take two steps with one foot to avoid swinging around the other foot on Up or Down.</li></ul><p>All styles check foot crossings, hold contacts and fast steps. The bot switches to the new footing straight away.</p><h2 id="when-the-footing-looks-wrong" tabindex="-1">When the footing looks wrong <a class="header-anchor" href="#when-the-footing-looks-wrong" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;When the footing looks wrong&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The bot&#39;s footing is a suggestion. Many patterns can be danced more than one way, and the bot shows one of them. If you&#39;d dance a step with the other foot, trust your body. To change a step for good, correct it in the chart editor, as described in <a href="./../parity.html#correcting-the-parity-checker">Correcting the parity checker</a>.</p>',27)])])}const c=e(r,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,c as default};
